{"findings":[{"severity":"critical","location":"Abstract","quote":"The completed programme comprises 787 runs in three stages. At GPU scale (314 runs; 25 matched seeds per variant on AG News, SST-2, and IMDB, plus ablations), standard scaling significantly outperformed both alternatives on AG News (+0.31 percentage points over 1/𝑑 𝑘 , 𝑑 𝑧 = 1.52, 𝑝 Holm < 0.001; +0.10 percentage points over 𝜏 = 2) and was indistinguishable on the binary tasks","issue":"The claim states that standard scaling significantly outperformed alternatives on AG News with 𝑑 𝑧 = 1.52, but a Cohen's d of 1.52 indicates an extremely large effect size that is inconsistent with the small performance difference of +0.31 percentage points. This suggests a possible miscalculation of the effect size or a reporting error.","suggestion":"Verify the calculation of Cohen's d (𝑑 𝑧) for the AG News comparison. If the effect size is indeed 1.52, report the actual performance difference that corresponds to this effect size. If the performance difference is correct as reported, recalculate and report the correct effect size."},{"severity":"major","location":"Abstract","quote":"the 𝑑 𝘨 grid shows no amplification (trend 𝑝 = 0.85), and early stopping selects the same epoch with indistinguishable learning curves for all variants.","issue":"The text mentions a "𝑑 𝘨 grid" but earlier in the abstract and introduction, the notation used is "𝑑 𝑘". This inconsistent notation could confuse readers and may indicate a typographical error.","suggestion":"Ensure consistent notation throughout the manuscript. If "𝑑 𝘨" is intended to be "𝑑 𝑘" (the dimensionality of the key vectors), correct all instances to use the same notation."},{"severity":"major","location":"Introduction","quote":"We implemented a compact pre-LN Transformer encoder [13, 14] whose attention module admits three scaling functions, verified by unit tests that the variants share bit-identical initialization and produce correctly masked computations","issue":"The claim that the variants produce "correctly masked computations" is not supported by any evidence or description of the verification process in the provided text. This is a critical claim for the validity of the experiment but lacks methodological detail.","suggestion":"Provide a brief description of the verification process for the masked computations, or reference a supplementary document where these details can be found."},{"severity":"minor","location":"Introduction","quote":"The preregistered hypothesis H1 — that at least one alternative scaling function would significantly outperform standard attention on a relevant metric — is partially falsified at the Stage-1 operating point","issue":"The text mentions a "preregistered hypothesis H1" but does not provide a registration number or link to where this hypothesis was preregistered. This lack of transparency makes it difficult to verify the claim of preregistration.","suggestion":"Include a registration number or link to the preregistration of hypothesis H1 to allow readers to verify the claim."},{"severity":"minor","location":"Introduction","quote":"The contribution of this paper is sixfold. (i) A clean, parameter-free experimental manipulation — a single divisor in one line of code — evaluated under a seed-matched paired design across 787 completed runs in three stages (GPU scale, replica completion, and an operating-point programme), with paired 𝑡-tests, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, Holm–Bonferroni correction, and effect sizes.","issue":"The text mentions "787 completed runs" but does not specify how many runs were conducted for each stage or dataset. This lack of detail makes it difficult to evaluate the scope and statistical power of the study.","suggestion":"Provide a breakdown of the number of runs conducted for each stage and dataset to allow readers to better understand the study's scope and statistical power."}]}
